%I A398628 #31 Sep 28 2026 22:01:35
%S A398628 1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,4,1,1,1,1,8,1,3,1,1,1,16,1,9,1,1,1,1,32,1,
%T A398628 27,1,4,1,1,1,64,1,81,1,16,2,1,1,1,128,1,243,1,64,4,3,1,1,1,256,1,729,
%U A398628 1,256,8,9,1,1,1,1,512,1,2187,1,1024,16,27,1,8
%N A398628 Square array T(n,k), n >= 0, k >= 1, read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) is the number of multiplicative perfect partitions of (p_1 * p_2 * ... * p_k)^n into parts > 1, for k distinct primes.
%C A398628 A multiplicative perfect partition of an integer N is a multiset of factors F = {f_1, ..., f_m} with f_i > 1 such that every divisor of N can be uniquely represented as the product of a submultiset of F.
%C A398628 For a number N = (p_1 * ... * p_k)^n having k distinct prime factors, a factor is called "mixed" if it is divisible by at least two distinct primes (e.g., p_1 * p_2). A factor is called "pure" if it is a power of a single prime (e.g., p_1^2).
%C A398628 No mixed factor can participate in a perfect partition of N. Consequently, every factor in F must be a pure prime power.
%C A398628 Because of this, the perfect partitions of N decouple into choosing independent 1-dimensional perfect partitions for each of the k prime-power components p_i^n. The number of perfect partitions of p^n is given by A002033(n), which is equivalent to the number of ordered factorizations of n+1 (A074206(n+1)).
%C A398628 Row n = 0 gives A000012. For n >= 1, row n is the all-1s sequence (A000012) if and only if n + 1 is prime (A000040).
%C A398628 Columns k = 1, 2, 3, 4 give the 1-dimensional (p^n, A002033), 2-dimensional ((p*q)^n), 3-dimensional ((p*q*r)^n), and 4-dimensional ((p*q*r*s)^n) perfect factorization diagonals respectively.
%C A398628 Cross-dimensional identity: T(n,4) = T(n,2)^2, and T(n,3) = sqrt(T(n,2) * T(n,4)) (the 3-dimensional count is the geometric mean of the 2-dimensional and 4-dimensional counts).
%C A398628 For any fixed n, row n is a geometric progression with common ratio r = A002033(n): T(n,k) = r^k. In particular, rows n = 3, 5, 7 give the powers of 2 (A000079), powers of 3 (A000244), and powers of 4 (A000302) respectively.
%C A398628 Column k = 2 gives the squares of A002033(n) (the 2-dimensional diagonal).
%D A398628 P. A. MacMahon, Combinatory Analysis, Cambridge Univ. Press, London and New York, Vol. 1, 1915 and Vol. 2, 1916.
%H A398628 Jishnu Babu Ranitha, <a href="/A398628/b398628.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%F A398628 T(n, k) = A002033(n)^k = A074206(n+1)^k.
%e A398628 Square array T(n,k) begins:
%e A398628 n\k |  1   2   3    4     5
%e A398628 ----+----------------------
%e A398628 0   |  1   1   1    1     1 ...
%e A398628 1   |  1   1   1    1     1 ...
%e A398628 2   |  1   1   1    1     1 ...
%e A398628 3   |  2   4   8   16    32 ...
%e A398628 4   |  1   1   1    1     1 ...
%e A398628 5   |  3   9  27   81   243 ...
%e A398628 6   |  1   1   1    1     1 ...
%e A398628 7   |  4  16  64  256  1024 ...
%e A398628 For n = 3: A074206(4) = 2. The 2 valid 1-dimensional bases for p^3 are {p, p, p} and {p, p^2}.
%e A398628 - For k = 1 (p^3): 2^1 = 2 factorizations: {p, p, p} and {p, p^2}.
%e A398628 - For k = 2 ((p*q)^3): 2^2 = 4 factorizations formed by combining {p, p, p} or {p, p^2} with {q, q, q} or {q, q^2}.
%t A398628 f[1]=1; f[n_]:=f[n]=Sum[f[d], {d, Most[Divisors[n]]}]; Flatten[Table[f[n+1]^(s-n), {s, 1, 12}, {n, 0, s-1}]]
%o A398628 (Python)
%o A398628 from sympy import divisors
%o A398628 from functools import lru_cache
%o A398628 @lru_cache(maxsize=None)
%o A398628 def ord_fact(n):
%o A398628     if n == 1: return 1
%o A398628     return sum(ord_fact(d) for d in divisors(n) if d < n)
%o A398628 def T(n, k):
%o A398628     return ord_fact(n + 1)**k
%o A398628 def antidiagonals(limit):
%o A398628     res = []
%o A398628     for s in range(1, limit + 1):
%o A398628         for n in range(s):
%o A398628             res.append(T(n, s - n))
%o A398628     return res
%o A398628 print(antidiagonals(12))
%Y A398628 Cf. A000012, A000040, A000079 (row 3), A000244 (row 5), A000302 (row 7), A002033, A074206, A325989.
%K A398628 nonn,tabl,easy,new
